      "page_title": "Cancellation, Tuning & Final Checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "11. Cancel & Detect",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const ac = new AbortController()",
            "await pipeline(src, gz, dst,",
            "  { signal: ac.signal })",
            "// ac.abort() → AbortError"
          ],
          "note": "abort rejects; every stage destroyed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "12. Tuning highWaterMark"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bigger: more throughput, fewer pauses, more memory",
            "Smaller: lower latency and memory",
            "Never raise it to **hide** a slow consumer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Implementation Checklist",
          "items": [
            "push(null) at EOF",
            "_flush if you buffer anything",
            "Call the callback exactly once",
            "Never assume chunk boundaries"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "When NOT To Stream",
          "items": [
            "Small files — readFile is clearer",
            "Data you need whole anyway: sort, JSON.parse",
            "Memory: buffered O(n), streamed O(highWaterMark)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One pipeline callback beats N pipe error listeners",
            "Always destroy(err); never emit('error')",
            "Web Streams interop: Readable.toWeb / fromWeb",
            "Async iterators cover many cases with no stream class"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Final rule",
          "text": "A stream is a memory-bounded pipe with a backwards feedback wire. Break the wire, break the memory bound."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of NODE.JS STREAMS. fs.readFileSync('10gb.log') puts 10 GB in your RSS. A read stream holds 64 KB — and that number does not change no matter how big the file gets. Ten pages on the four stream types, the drain protocol, why pipe leaks file descriptors and pipeline does not, and the twelve production traps that only show up under load. Save it for the next time a Node process gets OOM-killed at 3am.",
